  "title": "A bed the flitter can work",
  "question": "Does this read as a bee working a bed that can feed it?",
  "context_md": "The flitter's float landed this morning. It works -- it holds itself up, turns, and settles on the bloom -- and on your played bed it bought almost nothing, because a flitter there starts 138 columns from the nearest flowering clump and its eye reaches 32.\n\n**This is that bed with four more flowering clumps, placed just outside the bare band the nest needs.** It is the same mix otherwise -- grass, herb, shrub, the thicket and the tree -- and the colony still seats (28 / 28 / 32 founders against the plain bed's 36 / 24 / 35).\n\n**What it bought, over 120,000 frames on three seeds:** flowers drunk from goes 8 -> 74 at the median, **about nine times**, and young are born on two seeds instead of one. **What it did not buy is survival**: every flitter is dead by frame 30,000 on every bed I tried, and the flowers are still standing at the end.\n\n**And the reason is in this picture if you watch long enough.** On a bed this rich a flower is almost always inside the animal's sight, and the float only switches off when there is nothing in view -- so it barely comes down. 96% of its time in the air is powered, **55% of everything the colony eats goes on staying up**, and 29 of its 30 deaths happen in mid-air. That is a wiring number I can change; I have not changed it yet, because what I want to know first is whether this is the bed you would plant.",
